// A check the compiler counts as proof. An `if` whose branch always
// leaves tells the compiler what is true after it, so a construction the
// check makes safe needs no `attempt`. `double_order.jadpo` leaves the
// same limit to `CakeCount`'s own check instead.

/// The same order twice over, refused up front when that is too many.
action double_checked(count: CakeCount) fails OrderTooLarge -> CakeCount {
    // `reject` leaves, so below this `if` the compiler knows `count * 2`
    // is at most 24. `count` is at least 1, so `count * 2` is at least 2.
    // Neither `min: 1` nor `max: 24` can break, so `CakeCount(count * 2)`
    // is plain, and an `attempt` on it would be an error. The compiler
    // compares the expression as written: a check on `2 * count` wouldn't
    // count.
    if (count * 2 > 24) {
        reject OrderTooLarge
    }
    return CakeCount(count * 2)
}
